    "## Series\n",
    "\n",
    "Recall that in `R`, a `data.frame` is a collection of atomic vectors. In `pandas`, a `DataFrame` is a collection of `Series`.\n",
    "\n",
    "What is a `Series`? A `Series` is a one-dimensional object for storing data. E.g.:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "cec06453",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "0    1.0\n",
       "1    2.0\n",
       "2    3.0\n",
       "dtype: float64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 1,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "\n",
    "s1 = pd.Series([1.0, 2.0, 3.0])\n",
    "s1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b8941aee",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In many respects a `Series` behaves like an `R` vector. \n",
    "\n",
    "For example, we can compute statistics like the sum or the mean:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "59c52557",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "np.float64(6.0)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 2,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s1.sum()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "d05b3654",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "np.float64(2.0)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 3,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s1.mean()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "36177ddc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can do arithmetic with a `Series`, which will update each entry:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "c4df7b39",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "0    2.0\n",
       "1    3.0\n",
       "2    4.0\n",
       "dtype: float64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 4,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s1 + 1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "ee43c7e2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "0    1.0\n",
       "1    4.0\n",
       "2    9.0\n",
       "dtype: float64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 5,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s1**2 #(squaring)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "93e9e798",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Note that not all mathematical operations are built into base Python or `pandas`; for those operations, we will need to load an additional package, usually `NumPy`. For example, to take a square root:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "id": "d1ab6488",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "0    1.000000\n",
       "1    1.414214\n",
       "2    1.732051\n",
       "dtype: float64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 6,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "np.sqrt(s1)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "785811e5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "And we can do arithmetic with multiple `Series`:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "id": "2e4eaf4c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "0    5.0\n",
       "1    7.0\n",
       "2    9.0\n",
       "dtype: float64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 7,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s1 = pd.Series([1.0, 2.0, 3.0])\n",
    "s2 = pd.Series([4.0, 5.0, 6.0])\n",
    "\n",
    "s1 + s2"
   ]
