   "id": "db59399d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "0   -1.0\n",
       "1    3.0\n",
       "2    0.5\n",
       "3    0.9\n",
       "dtype: float64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 11,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "y"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0c25470a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
    "## Question\n",
    "\n",
    "Compute the correlation between the two series, using the formula and the arithmetic operations described above. (The answer should be approximately -0.168)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "41b44752",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "np.float64(-0.016752915420927886)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 12,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "#| include: false\n",
    "\n",
    "sum((x - x.mean()) * (y - y.mean())) / (\n",
    "  np.sqrt((x - x.mean()).pow(2).sum()) * \n",
    "  np.sqrt((y - y.mean()).pow(2).sum())\n",
    ")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f19c19e0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Indexes\n",
    "\n",
    "We said that a `Series` behaves, in some ways, like an atomic vector in `R`. However, there are some important differences.\n",
    "\n",
    "One **key difference** is that a `Series` always has an **Index** -- this is like a label for each entry in the `Series`. The Index can be specified when creating the Series:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "id": "d01cbec1",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "a    1\n",
       "b    2\n",
       "c    3\n",
       "dtype: int64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 13,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s1 = pd.Series([1, 2, 3], index = ['a', 'b', 'c'])\n",
    "s1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8478d3d1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Important:** The `a, b, c` that is printed on the left is **not** another column in the `Series`. Rather, this is the Index!\n",
    "\n",
    "If we don't specify an index, `pandas` will default to a range index starting at 0:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 14,
   "id": "9e57c14d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "0    hello\n",
       "1    world\n",
       "dtype: object"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 14,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s2 = pd.Series([\"hello\", \"world\"])\n",
    "s2"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9958f6cf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Choosing entries with the Index\n",
    "\n",
    "The Index can be used to choose entries from the `Series`:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "id": "c5ab1710",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "np.int64(1)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 15,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s1['a']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 16,
   "id": "a8ca3c85",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "a    1\n",
       "b    2\n",
       "dtype: int64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 16,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s1[['a', 'b']]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "38cf2ee4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Notes:**\n",
    "\n",
    "* If a single entry is selected, we **reduce the dimensionality** of the resulting object. That is, `s1['a']` returns a single number (the integer `1`), rather than a `Series`\n",
    "* To select multiple entries, we usually need to include them in a Python `list`. That is: `s1[['a', 'b']]` will work, but `s1['a', 'b']` will not!\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
    "## Question\n",
    "\n",
    "Verify that running the following code does not work:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
   "id": "07d5c850",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#| eval: false\n",
    "s1 = pd.Series([1, 2, 3], index = ['a', 'b', 'c'])\n",
    "s1['a', 'b']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7bed58a5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
    "## Question\n",
    "\n",
    "Consider the following `Series`:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 18,
   "id": "b88a17ba",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "x = pd.Series([\"Statistics\", \"Mathematics\", \"Computer Science\", \"Spanish\"], \n",
    "              index = ['u', 'v', 'y', 'z'])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5c44debb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Extract just \"Statistics\" and \"Mathematics\", using the Index labels.\n",
    "\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Positional indexing\n",
    "\n",
    "Indexes in `pandas` often feel weird to `R` users, because we usually don't have row names for our vectors or data frames (it is sometimes possible, but it is not common). In `R`, we are instead used to indexing our vectors by *position*.\n",
    "\n",
    "Fortunately, using `.iloc` we can index a `Series` by position too!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 19,
   "id": "aa6c7fe8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "np.int64(11)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 18,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s1 = pd.Series([11, 23, 35], index = ['a', 'b', 'c'])\n",
    "s1.iloc[0]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 20,
   "id": "64400978",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "np.int64(23)"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 19,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s1.iloc[1]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 21,
   "id": "d37a7dd8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "b    23\n",
       "dtype: int64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 20,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s1.iloc[1:2] # right endpoint is not inlcuded!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 22,
   "id": "d79914c9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "b    23\n",
       "c    35\n",
       "dtype: int64"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 21,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "s1.iloc[1:3]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3257b4dc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Notes:**\n",
    "\n",
    "* **In Python, positional indexing begins at 0!!** (whereas in `R`, it begins at 1)\n",
    "* In `R`, doing `1:3` will include both endpoints in the slice. In `Python`, the left endpoint will be included, but the right endpoint will *not*\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
    "## Question\n",
    "\n",
    "Consider the following `Series`:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 23,
   "id": "c186b16e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "x = pd.Series([\"Statistics\", \"Mathematics\", \"Computer Science\", \"Spanish\"], \n",
    "              index = ['u', 'v', 'y', 'z'])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "24a5337d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Extract just \"Statistics\" and \"Mathematics\", using *positional* indexing.\n",
    "\n",
    ":::\n",
